From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: LLCOOLT <114613597+LLCOOLT@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 22:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] gx: texture upscaling off the frame's thread

A player (RTX 4090) saw the game chug with Texture upscaling, mostly in
cutscenes with a Paper Mario 64 ROM linked. Each texture missing from the
content cache was decoded, upscaled (the application's filter: 5-300 ms a
texture at 4x), given its levels and uploaded on the frame's thread, so a scene
that loads a cast of textures at once (a cutscene's, Paper Mario 64's sprite
rasters as their animations reach new frames) stalled for frames on end: the
prologue at 4x spent 9.6 s of its frames upscaling, one frame 2.1 s. At 4x the
128 MiB content cache also held a fraction of a room's textures, so they were
evicted and upscaled again as it played (Rogueport's plaza with the Paper
Mario 64 bosses: 378 of 688 upscales the same picture again).

- A texture missing from the content cache is uploaded as it is, at once, and
  drawn so until its upscaled picture is in. Its bytes (and its palette's) go
  to Aurora's upscale workers (a quarter of the CPU's threads, 1 to 4, low
  priority), which decode, upscale and make the levels there.
- Results go in at a frame's end (the FIFO drained): the texture made (one a
  frame) and uploaded a megabyte a frame at most within 1.5 ms, then put in the
  content cache under the same key and in each texture object that held the
  picture drawn meanwhile, the bindings invalidated. A picture is upscaled once.
  One not drawn in the last 2 frames is kept back until it is drawn again.
- A source (its address, palette, size and format) whose picture changed 3
  times, each within 30 frames of the last, is taken as drawn into for 600
  frames: its new pictures are not given to the workers; one of them seen again
  (a content cache hit) is then. So is one put off while the workers had 512
  pictures or 32 MiB waiting.
- The content cache's budget is its own (halved when video memory runs out)
  times the upscaling's factor; the memory guards step the upscaling down, and
  this with it, when memory runs short.
- A change of the factor or the filter drops what the workers were given.
- AuroraSetTextureUpscaleWait(1) (tests on a virtual clock): each texture is
  upscaled on the frame's thread where it is first drawn, as before, and a
  frame's end waits for anything the workers still have, so captures are the
  same whatever the machine's speed (the prologue's at 4x: the same pixels).
- AuroraGetTextureUpscaleStats: the last frame's work (time on the frame's
  thread, handed out, put in, waiting, sources taken as changing).
- One thread (the browser without threads): upscaled where it is asked for, put
  in at the frame's end as the workers' are.
- LruCache::peek: a value and how long since it was used, without touching it.
---
 include/aurora/upscale.h |  25 ++
 lib/gfx/lru_cache.hpp    |  12 +
 lib/gx/texture.cpp       | 583 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 3 files changed, 589 insertions(+), 31 deletions(-)

diff --git a/include/aurora/upscale.h b/include/aurora/upscale.h
index 054995a..e1745cf 100644
--- a/include/aurora/upscale.h
+++ b/include/aurora/upscale.h
@@ -13,11 +13,36 @@ extern "C" {
  * times larger: its first level decoded to RGBA8 and given to upscaler (out: factor*width x factor*height
  * RGBA8), the smaller levels averaged down from the result. factor 1 or upscaler NULL: off. Applies to
  * textures loaded after the call.
+ *
+ * The upscaler runs on worker threads of Aurora's own (several at once: it must be reentrant), never on the
+ * frame's: a texture is drawn as it is until its upscaled picture is ready, which goes in at a frame's end
+ * (a few each frame, within a time budget), once per picture (textures are kept by their content). A texture
+ * whose picture keeps changing (one drawn into each frame) is drawn as it is; each of its pictures that comes
+ * back is upscaled then.
  */
 typedef void (*AuroraTextureUpscaler)(const unsigned char* rgba, unsigned width, unsigned height, unsigned factor,
                                       unsigned char* out);
 void AuroraSetTextureUpscale(unsigned factor, AuroraTextureUpscaler upscaler);
 
+/**
+ * wait 1 (tests on a virtual clock: the same pictures whatever the machine's speed): each texture upscaled on the
+ * frame's thread where it is first drawn, and drawn so from its first frame, as before the workers; a frame's end
+ * also waits for anything the workers still have. 0 (the default): never waits.
+ */
+void AuroraSetTextureUpscaleWait(int wait);
+
+/** The upscaling's work in the last frame (tests and the performance log). */
+typedef struct AuroraTextureUpscaleStats {
+  unsigned syncMicros;               // time the frame's own thread spent on it (decoding, handing out, putting in)
+  unsigned queued;                   // textures given to the upscaling workers
+  unsigned published;                // upscaled textures put in place of the ones drawn meanwhile
+  unsigned long long publishedBytes; // their size
+  unsigned waiting;                  // given to the workers and not in yet (as the frame ended)
+  unsigned dynamicSkips;             // textures drawn as they are: their picture keeps changing (drawn into)
+  unsigned deferred;                 // pictures drawn as they are until they are drawn again (all in all)
+} AuroraTextureUpscaleStats;
+void AuroraGetTextureUpscaleStats(AuroraTextureUpscaleStats* out);
+
 #ifdef __cplusplus
 }
 #endif
